//Error Description:  
/**The program is running out of memory because it tries to load and hold too many large images in RAM at once.
In plain terms: the computer can’t fit all the images into memory, so it crashes.
*/

//Root Cause:
/**
The code loads all images into memory simultaneously (List<BufferedImage> images).
It then creates another list (processedImages) that also holds all processed versions.
Each BufferedImage can be very large (millions of pixels), so storing hundreds or thousands at once quickly exceeds the heap limit.
*/
//Specific Memory-Intensive Operations
/** 
ImageIO.read(imageFile) → loads entire image into memory.
images.add(image) → keeps every original image in memory.
processedImages.add(processed) → doubles memory usage (original + processed).
Nested pixel loops in applyEffects → create new BufferedImage objects for every image, further increasing memory usage.
*/
//Practical Ways to Reduce Memory Consumption
/**
-Process images one by one
-Don’t store all images in lists. Instead:

java
for (File imageFile : imageFiles) {
    BufferedImage image = ImageIO.read(imageFile);
    BufferedImage processed = applyEffects(image);
    ImageIO.write(processed, getImageFormat(imageFile.getName()), new File(outputName));
}
This way, only one image is in memory at a time.

-Release references early
-After saving, set references to null so garbage collection can reclaim memory.

Example:

java
image = null;
processed = null;
Use streaming or scaling

If images are very large, consider scaling them down before processing.

Or use libraries that support streaming image processing (e.g., Apache Commons Imaging).
*/
