Bottlenecks aren’t always CPU‑bound; memory can be the limiting factor.
Storing intermediate results “just in case” often leads to waste.
The JVM heap limit is a hard boundary — exceeding it means failure, not slowdown.
I learned that profiling tools reveal hidden costs (like duplicate lists of large objects) that aren’t obvious from reading code.
*/

